\title{Records as queries}
\import{prelude}

\p{The simplest use of a record as a query is to produce the cartesian product of the constituent queries. The SQL equivalent here is a \code{CROSS JOIN}.}

\pre{%
\kw{theory} Graph := \kw{sig}
  V : Set
  E : V -> V -> Set
\kw{end}

\kw{def} all-pairs (m : Graph) : \kw{Set} := \kw{sig}
  first-vertex : m.V
  second-vertex : m.V
\kw{end}
}

\p{The power of record types as queries comes when exploiting dependency. For example, we can query for the set of all edges in the graph:}

\pre{
\kw{def} all-edges (m : Graph) : \kw{Set} := \kw{sig}
  a : m.V
  b : m.V
  e : m.E a b
\kw{end}
}

\p{Or, more interestingly, all of the self-loops:}

\pre{
\kw{def} all-loops (m : Graph) : \kw{Set} := \kw{sig}
  a : m.V
  e : m.E a a
\kw{end}
}

\p{Or all of the triangles:}

\pre{
\kw{def} all-triangles (m : Graph) : \kw{Set} := \kw{sig}
  a : m.V
  b : m.V
  c : m.V
  f : m.E a b
  g : m.E b c
  h : m.E c a
\kw{end}
}

\collapsedaside{\taxon{Aside} There is a sense in which the fields in a theory that end in \code{Set} are specifying the "atomic queries" that it is possible to run on the corresponding database. The more complex queries on \code{Graph} above are all specified by composing the simpler ones. In fact, in an earlier version of Coln, \code{Set} was written as \code{Query} to make this clear.}
